libumem - User Guide and Examples
=================================

This document covers how to build, use, and debug applications with
libumem.  It includes a complete example of integrating umem as the
memory allocator for a PostgreSQL-style memory context system.


1. Overview
-----------

libumem is a slab-based memory allocator originally developed by Sun
Microsystems for Solaris.  This port brings the same allocator to
Linux, FreeBSD, macOS, and other POSIX systems.

Key properties:

  - Slab allocation with per-CPU magazines for low contention
  - Object caching (constructor/destructor callbacks)
  - Built-in debugging: buffer auditing, redzone checking, guard
    pages, allocation stack traces
  - Per-thread caching for small allocations
  - Environment-variable-driven debug modes (no recompilation needed)
  - Scoped arena allocator for temporary allocations
  - Ownership/borrowing system inspired by Rust
  - Conservative garbage collector with Boehm-compatible API
  - Allocation profiling with phase detection

The primary advantage over malloc/free is that umem detects memory
corruption at the point it happens rather than letting it silently
corrupt unrelated data.  The debug modes add overhead, but the release
mode is competitive with system malloc.

NOTE: Ownership (umem_own.h), garbage collection (umem_gc.h, gc.h),
allocation profiling (umem_profile.h), and budget contexts
(umem_palloc.h) are EXPERIMENTAL APIs.  They may change without
notice.  External code must #define UMEM_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL before
including these headers.


2. Building
-----------

From the repository root:

    ./configure
    make -j$(nproc)
    make check

To install system-wide:

    sudo make install
    sudo ldconfig

To use without installing (for testing or development):

    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=.libs ./your_app

To intercept malloc/free in an existing application:

    LD_PRELOAD=.libs/libumem_malloc.so ./your_app

This replaces the system allocator with umem for the target process
without any code changes.


3. Basic Usage
--------------

There are two allocation interfaces: raw allocation and object caches.

Raw allocation (drop-in replacement for malloc/free):

    #include <umem.h>

    void *p = umem_alloc(256, UMEM_DEFAULT);
    if (p == NULL) {
        /* handle allocation failure */
    }
    /* use p */
    umem_free(p, 256);  /* caller must supply size */

    void *z = umem_zalloc(1024, UMEM_DEFAULT);  /* zero-filled */
    umem_free(z, 1024);

The UMEM_NOFAIL flag can be used instead of UMEM_DEFAULT to make
allocations that never return NULL (they call exit() on failure
instead).  Register a callback with umem_nofail_callback() to
customize this behavior.

Object caches (for fixed-size objects allocated repeatedly):

    static umem_cache_t *widget_cache;

    /* Create once during initialization */
    widget_cache = umem_cache_create("widget_cache",
        sizeof(widget_t),  /* object size */
        0,                 /* alignment (0 = default) */
        widget_constructor,  /* called on new slab objects */
        widget_destructor,   /* called when slab is destroyed */
        NULL,              /* reclaim callback (optional) */
        NULL,              /* callback argument */
        NULL,              /* vmem source (NULL = default) */
        0);                /* flags */

    /* Fast allocation from cache */
    widget_t *w = umem_cache_alloc(widget_cache, UMEM_DEFAULT);
    /* ... use w ... */
    umem_cache_free(widget_cache, w);

    /* Destroy when done */
    umem_cache_destroy(widget_cache);

Object caches are the core of umem's performance.  Objects are kept
in magazines (per-CPU freelists), so allocation and free are often
just a pointer swap with no locking.


4. Debug Modes
--------------

All debug modes are controlled by the UMEM_DEBUG environment variable.
No recompilation is needed.  Set the variable before your application
starts.

  UMEM_DEBUG=default

    Enables the full debugging suite: audit (stack traces), contents
    logging, and guards (deadbeef patterns + redzone checking).  This
    is the recommended setting during development.

    Overhead: 2-5x slower allocations, significant memory overhead
    for stack trace storage.

    What it catches: buffer overflows, use-after-free, double-free,
    uninitialized memory reads, memory leaks (via stack traces).

  UMEM_DEBUG=audit

    Enables allocation/free stack trace recording.  Each buffer gets
    a transaction record with the stack trace of the code that last
    allocated or freed it.

    Overhead: ~2x slower allocations due to stack unwinding.

    What it catches: memory leaks (you can identify who allocated
    leaked buffers), use-after-free (the free stack trace shows who
    freed it).

    You can control the stack depth:  UMEM_DEBUG=audit=20
    records 20 frames instead of the default.

  UMEM_DEBUG=guards

    Enables deadbeef pattern filling and redzone checking.  Freed
    memory is filled with 0xdeadbeef.  A redzone sentinel (0xfeedface)
    is placed after each allocation.  On every free, umem verifies
    that the patterns are intact.

    Overhead: ~1.5x slower frees due to pattern verification.

    What it catches: buffer overflows (redzone corruption), writes
    to freed memory (deadbeef pattern corruption), partial
    double-frees.

  UMEM_DEBUG=firewall=<minbytes>

    Places every allocation of at least <minbytes> bytes so that
    its end is adjacent to an unmapped page.  Any buffer overflow
    immediately triggers a segfault.

    Overhead: one page of virtual memory wasted per allocation.

    What it catches: buffer overflows (immediately, at the point
    of the overflowing write, not at the next free).

    Example:  UMEM_DEBUG=firewall=64
    puts a guard page after every allocation >= 64 bytes.

  UMEM_DEBUG=lite

    Lightweight checking with minimal overhead.  Suitable for
    production use when you need some safety net but cannot afford
    the full debug cost.

Options can be combined:

    UMEM_DEBUG=audit,guards
    UMEM_DEBUG=audit=16,firewall=128,verbose


5. Stack Traces on Error
-------------------------

When umem detects memory corruption (with UMEM_DEBUG=audit or
UMEM_DEBUG=default), it prints a diagnostic message to stderr
that includes GDB-style stack traces:

    umem: buffer 0x7f4a8c001200 (size 64, cache "umem_alloc_64"):
      previous transaction was a free:
        #0  0x7f4a8a012e3a in umem_cache_free+0x1a ()
        #1  0x7f4a8a012f4b in umem_free+0x4b ()
        #2  0x00000040123c in process_query+0x8c () at handler.c:142
        #3  0x000000401056 in main+0x56 () at server.c:38

To get file:line information in the stack traces, compile your
application with debug symbols:

    gcc -g -O2 -o myapp myapp.c -lumem

The stack traces use the libumem built-in unwinder (based on
libunwind or frame pointers, depending on platform).  For best
results, avoid -fomit-frame-pointer.


6. PostgreSQL Integration Example
----------------------------------

The files in this directory demonstrate how to integrate umem with
PostgreSQL's MemoryContext system using budget-based memory contexts.
See:

    umem_palloc.h    Public header
    umem_palloc.c    Implementation and demo

Each UmemBudgetContext wraps a vmem arena.  A budget caps how much
memory the arena can dispense.  When the budget is exhausted, callers
either block (backpressure), get NULL, or abort, depending on flags.


6.1 Memory Budgets
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Each context is created with a maximum byte budget:

    UmemBudgetContext *top = umem_budget_create(
        "TopMemoryContext", 512 * 1024 * 1024, 0);

The budget is enforced on every allocation.  vmem_size(arena,
VMEM_ALLOC) is checked against the budget before each vmem_alloc().
If the allocation would exceed the budget, the allocator tries
umem_reap() to reclaim cached memory, then applies backpressure
or returns NULL depending on flags.


6.2 Backpressure
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

When a context's budget is exceeded:

  - Default (no flags): the calling thread waits on a condition
    variable with a 100ms timeout, retrying up to 50 times.  Each
    umem_budget_free() signals the condition variable, waking
    blocked allocators.

  - UMEM_BUDGET_NOWAIT: return NULL immediately.

  - UMEM_BUDGET_NOFAIL: abort() with a diagnostic message.

This models PostgreSQL's behavior where query execution should block
rather than fail when the shared buffer pool is full, while DDL
operations might prefer NOWAIT.


6.3 Pre-allocation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Use UMEM_BUDGET_PREALLOC for latency-sensitive contexts:

    UmemBudgetContext *hot = umem_budget_create(
        "HotPath", 64 * 1024, UMEM_BUDGET_PREALLOC);

This mmaps the entire budget with MAP_POPULATE, faulting in all
pages at creation time.  The vmem arena is created on this fixed
backing region with no import function, so it cannot grow.
Allocations never trigger page faults.

Without PREALLOC, the arena grows on demand via the default vmem
heap.  The budget is still enforced, but page faults may occur.


6.4 Shared Memory
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

For multi-process PostgreSQL deployments, shared memory contexts
let multiple backends allocate from the same pool:

    /* Postmaster creates the segment */
    UmemBudgetContext *shm = umem_shared_create(
        "shared_buffers", 128 * 1024 * 1024, 0);

    /* After fork, each backend attaches */
    UmemBudgetContext *my_view = umem_shared_attach(
        "shared_buffers");

    /* Use it */
    void *buf = umem_budget_alloc(my_view, 8192);
    /* ... */
    umem_budget_free(my_view, buf, 8192);

    /* Backend detaches (does not unlink the segment) */
    umem_shared_detach(my_view);

This uses POSIX shared memory (shm_open/ftruncate/mmap MAP_SHARED).
The vmem arena metadata is per-process, so each attached process
gets its own vmem handle over the shared backing.

Note: cross-process vmem_alloc coordination requires external
locking.  This demo shows the single-process attach/detach path.


6.5 Hierarchy
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Contexts form parent/child trees.  A child's allocations count
against both its own budget and all ancestor budgets:

    UmemBudgetContext *top = umem_budget_create(
        "TopMemCtx", 512 * 1024 * 1024,
        UMEM_BUDGET_PREALLOC);

    UmemBudgetContext *exec = umem_budget_create_child(
        top, "ExecutorCtx", 64 * 1024 * 1024,
        UMEM_BUDGET_AUDIT);

    UmemBudgetContext *tuple = umem_budget_create_child(
        exec, "TupleCtx", 16 * 1024 * 1024,
        UMEM_BUDGET_GUARDS);

Deleting a parent recursively deletes all children.  Resetting a
context destroys and recreates its arena; child contexts are not
affected (they have their own arenas).


6.6 Per-Context Debugging
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Different contexts can have different debug levels:

    UMEM_BUDGET_AUDIT    enable allocation stack traces
    UMEM_BUDGET_GUARDS   enable redzone + deadbeef patterns
    UMEM_BUDGET_OWN      enable ownership tracking

This lets you enable heavy debugging on a suspected subsystem
while keeping production-level overhead on the rest.


To build and run the demo:

    cd examples
    make
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=../.libs ./umem_palloc_demo

To see debug diagnostics:

    UMEM_DEBUG=default LD_LIBRARY_PATH=../.libs ./umem_palloc_demo


7. GDB/LLDB Debugging Commands
-------------------------------

When debugging a core dump or live process that uses libumem, these
GDB commands are useful.

Print the global umem flags (shows which debug modes are active):

    (gdb) print umem_flags

Walk the cache list (all umem caches are in a linked list):

    (gdb) set $cp = umem_null_cache.cache_next
    (gdb) while $cp != &umem_null_cache
     > printf "cache: %s  size: %d  alloc: %llu  free: %llu\n", \
     >   $cp->cache_name, $cp->cache_bufsize, \
     >   $cp->cache_alloc, $cp->cache_free
     > set $cp = $cp->cache_next
     > end

Inspect a specific buffer's buftag (when guards are active):

    (gdb) set $bt = (umem_buftag_t *)((char *)$buf + $cache_bufsize)
    (gdb) print/x *$bt

    The bt_redzone field should be 0xfeedface.  If it is not, the
    buffer was overflowed.

Get the allocation stack trace for a buffer (when audit is active):

    (gdb) set $bcp = (umem_bufctl_audit_t *)$bt->bt_bufctl
    (gdb) print $bcp->bc_depth
    (gdb) print $bcp->bc_stack[0]@$bcp->bc_depth

    Then use "info symbol <address>" to resolve each frame.

In LLDB, the equivalent commands use "expr" instead of "print" and
"p" instead of "set":

    (lldb) expr umem_flags
    (lldb) p (umem_buftag_t *)((char *)buf + cache_bufsize)


8. Allocation Profiling
------------------------

libumem can record allocation profiles that capture per-cache
allocation rates and detect behavioral phases in your application.

Record a profile:

    UMEM_OPTIONS=profile=record:/tmp/myapp.ump ./myapp

This creates a binary .ump file containing timestamped allocation
counts for each cache.

Analyze a profile:

    tools/umem_profile_dump /tmp/myapp.ump

The dump tool shows:

  - Per-cache allocation counts and rates
  - Detected phases (startup, steady-state, shutdown)
  - Phase transitions with timestamps
  - Thread-level allocation breakdowns

Use a recorded profile to pre-warm caches on startup:

    UMEM_OPTIONS=profile=use:/tmp/myapp.ump ./myapp

This pre-creates magazines based on the recorded steady-state
allocation pattern, reducing warmup time.

Profiling adds approximately 3-5% overhead to allocation-heavy
workloads.  It is designed to be safe for production use.


9. Ownership Tracking
----------------------

libumem includes an ownership/borrowing system inspired by Rust's
ownership model.  It tracks who owns each allocation and detects
common memory safety violations at runtime.

Basic usage:

    #include <umem_own.h>

    /* Allocate with ownership tracking */
    umem_owned_t *buf = umem_own_alloc(256, UMEM_DEFAULT);

    /* Get a pointer to the data */
    void *ptr = umem_deref(buf);
    memcpy(ptr, data, 256);

    /* Borrow a read-only reference */
    umem_ref_t ref = umem_borrow(buf);
    const void *rptr = ref.ref_data;
    /* ... read from rptr ... */
    umem_unborrow(ref);

    /* Borrow a mutable reference (exclusive) */
    umem_ref_t mref = umem_borrow_mut(buf);
    void *mptr = mref.ref_data;
    /* ... write to mptr ... */
    umem_unborrow(mref);

    /* Transfer ownership */
    umem_owned_t *new_owner = umem_move(buf);
    /* buf is now invalid; using it triggers a violation */

    /* Release the allocation */
    umem_drop(new_owner);

Two modes of operation:

  Lightweight (always on when using umem_own_alloc):
    Tracks ownership state and reference counts.  Approximately 2%
    overhead.  Catches double-free and use-after-free.

  Full debug (UMEM_OPTIONS=ownership):
    Adds thread affinity checks, borrow conflict detection, and
    stack capture on alloc.  Approximately 15% overhead.  Catches
    data races (concurrent mutable borrows), cross-thread ownership
    violations, and leaked allocations at shutdown.

Violations detected:

    UMEM_OWN_USE_AFTER_FREE     Dereference after umem_drop()
    UMEM_OWN_USE_AFTER_MOVE     Dereference after umem_move()
    UMEM_OWN_DOUBLE_FREE        Calling umem_drop() twice
    UMEM_OWN_BORROW_CONFLICT    Mutable borrow while other borrows exist
    UMEM_OWN_THREAD_VIOLATION   Access from wrong thread (debug mode)
    UMEM_OWN_LEAK               Owned allocation not freed at shutdown

Register a custom error handler:

    void my_handler(umem_own_error_t err,
        const umem_owned_t *owned, const char *msg) {
        fprintf(stderr, "ownership violation: %s\n", msg);
        abort();
    }
    umem_own_set_error_handler(my_handler);


10. Garbage Collection
-----------------------

libumem includes a conservative garbage collector with a Boehm GC
compatible API.  It uses umem's slab allocator as the backing store,
so you get umem's debugging features on GC-managed memory too.

Basic usage:

    #include <umem_gc.h>

    /* Initialize once */
    GC_INIT();

    /* Allocate (may contain pointers -- will be scanned) */
    void *p = GC_MALLOC(256);

    /* Allocate atomic (no pointers -- skip scanning) */
    char *s = GC_MALLOC_ATOMIC(128);

    /* Explicit free (optional -- GC will collect it) */
    GC_FREE(p);

    /* Force a collection cycle */
    GC_gcollect();

For multi-threaded applications, each thread must register:

    /* In each thread's entry function */
    GC_register_my_thread();
    /* ... do work ... */
    GC_unregister_my_thread();

Finalizers (called before the object is collected):

    void my_finalizer(void *obj, void *client_data) {
        close(*(int *)obj);  /* close a file descriptor */
    }
    GC_REGISTER_FINALIZER(p, my_finalizer, NULL, NULL, NULL);

Hybrid usage -- mix manual and collected allocations:

    /* Manual (fast, deterministic lifetime) */
    widget_t *w = umem_cache_alloc(widget_cache, UMEM_DEFAULT);
    /* ... */
    umem_cache_free(widget_cache, w);

    /* Collected (convenient, no explicit free needed) */
    node_t *n = GC_MALLOC(sizeof(node_t));
    n->left = GC_MALLOC(sizeof(node_t));
    /* n and n->left will be collected when unreachable */

The GC uses a mark-sweep algorithm with concurrent marking and
stop-the-world remark and finalize phases.


11. Arena Allocator
--------------------

libumem provides a scoped arena allocator for temporary allocation
patterns where many allocations share a common lifetime (parsers,
request handlers, query processing).

    #include <umem.h>

    /* Create an arena with 64KB of backing memory */
    umem_arena_t *arena = umem_arena_create(65536, UMEM_DEFAULT);

    /* Bump-pointer allocation (16-byte aligned, very fast) */
    char *buf = umem_arena_alloc(arena, 1024);
    int *ids = umem_arena_alloc(arena, 100 * sizeof(int));

    /* Check remaining space */
    size_t avail = umem_arena_available(arena);

    /* Reset: instant bulk free (sets offset back to 0) */
    umem_arena_reset(arena);
    /* All previous pointers from this arena are now invalid */

    /* Allocate again from the reset arena */
    char *buf2 = umem_arena_alloc(arena, 512);

    /* Destroy releases all backing memory */
    umem_arena_destroy(arena);

Arenas are not thread-safe.  Each thread should use its own arena.
The backing memory is obtained via mmap and returned via munmap.

Arenas are ideal for request-scoped allocation in servers: create
an arena per request, allocate freely during processing, then reset
or destroy the arena when the request is complete.


12. Environment Variables Reference
-------------------------------------

UMEM_DEBUG

    Controls debug checking on allocations.

    default         Enable audit, contents, and guards
    audit[=frames]  Record stack traces (default depth: 15 frames)
    contents[=bytes]  Store freed object contents (with UMEM_LOGGING)
    guards          Enable deadbeef fill and redzone checking
    firewall=bytes  Guard pages for allocations >= bytes
    lite            Lightweight checking
    verbose         Print error messages to stderr
    nosignal        Abort on allocations from signal handlers
    noabort         Do not abort on recoverable errors
    mtbf=N          Inject allocation failures (mean time between)
    random          Randomize debug flags per cache

    Multiple options separated by commas:
        UMEM_DEBUG=audit=20,guards,verbose

UMEM_OPTIONS

    Controls allocator tuning and optional features.

    perthread_cache=bytes   Size of per-thread cache (default: varies)
    tcache=0|1              Enable/disable per-thread caching
    tcache_max=bytes        Maximum object size for per-thread cache
    reclaim=0|1             Enable background slab reclamation
    reclaim_delay=seconds   Delay before dirty slabs are reclaimed
    ownership=0|1           Enable full ownership/borrowing debug
    profile=record:/path    Record allocation profile to file
    profile=use:/path       Use recorded profile for cache warmup
    sbrk_minalloc=bytes     Minimum sbrk heap allocation chunk
    sbrk_pagesize=bytes     Preferred sbrk heap page size

    Multiple options separated by commas:
        UMEM_OPTIONS=tcache=1,perthread_cache=65536,reclaim=1

UMEM_LOGGING

    Controls internal transaction logging.

    transaction[=size]  Log allocation/free transactions
    contents[=size]     Log freed object contents
    fail[=size]         Log failed allocations
    slab[=size]         Log slab creation events

    The size parameter sets the log buffer size in bytes.

    Multiple options separated by commas:
        UMEM_LOGGING=transaction,contents


